1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Welcome to JSON-SCADA, an open platform for monitoring and operating industrial systems.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The Display Viewer presents a substation as a single-line diagram with live electrical measurements.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Equipment symbols, voltage levels, and power flows provide a geographic and operational context.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The top toolbar selects screens and provides zoom, pan, fit, slideshow, and time-machine controls.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Measurements appear beside transformers, breakers, buses, and outgoing feeders.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The alarm ticker at the top keeps recent conditions visible while an operator views the diagram.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
The Alarms Viewer gathers active conditions across the monitored system.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Rows show each station, the point description, its state, quality indicators, and timestamps.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Filters and column controls help operators focus on the alarms that matter to their work.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Colors and status text make it easier to distinguish conditions in a busy alarm list.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The toolbar offers acknowledgement and audible alarm controls for authorized operators.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
This walkthrough is read-only: no alarm acknowledgements or field commands are issued.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
The Tabular Viewer offers a point-by-point view of a selected station.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Here AGGAN CT11 is selected, showing measurements and equipment states in one table.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Rows include values and units, quality information, alarm time, and source time.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Station and bay selectors narrow a large system to a useful operational scope.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Commandable and abnormal filters help operators find specific kinds of points.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The example includes analog measurements and open or closed equipment states.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
The Events Viewer displays a chronological stream of changes and events.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Timestamps and point descriptions help reconstruct what happened and where.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Event history complements the active alarm view when investigating an incident.

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
CIM-EMS adds network analysis based on an imported CGMES model.

23
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
The demo includes the active SVEDALA model, revision 1, using CGMES version 3.0.

24
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
The model list displays import metadata and counts of errors, warnings, and unsupported items.

25
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Bindings connect network model elements to SCADA tags.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
This screen lists switch and analog bindings, their associated tags, and approval state.

27
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Search and kind filters support review of mappings in large network models.

28
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The Runs view shows repeated analysis cycles and their results.

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Each row reports its trigger, status, processing time, and model revision.

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The visible state-estimation results converged; contingency-analysis status appears alongside them.

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
The Requests view is where analysis requests and their status can be inspected.

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Its columns include request type, user, status, node, completion time, and errors.

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
There are no requests in this captured view, so the table is empty.

34
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:24,000
The Admin dashboard brings together users, roles, drivers, connections, and tags.

35
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The Users tab shows account details, assigned roles, and authentication information.

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Search, refresh, and CSV export controls help administrators review the configured accounts.

37
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Roles define the permissions available to groups of users.

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
The role list summarizes rights and provides access to role configuration.

39
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Access controls matter: the CIM-EMS screen shown earlier explicitly reports read-only access.

40
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Driver Instances lists the protocol processes configured for this installation.

41
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The table summarizes driver names, status, instance numbers, and associated connections.

42
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
JSON-SCADA integrates different protocols through separate communication drivers.

43
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Connections describe individual links between drivers and external systems or devices.

44
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The table presents status, connection names, endpoints, and other operational details.

45
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Status counts across the top help administrators spot connections needing attention.

46
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
The Tags view exposes the point database behind displays, alarms, and integrations.

47
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Search and filter controls help locate tags among a large set of measurements and states.

48
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
The list includes tag identity, grouping, descriptions, and current configuration.

49
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The System tab groups project backup and process-management controls.

50
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
It also displays system totals and administrative tools for maintaining the installation.

51
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,000
These controls can change the system; this demonstration only shows the interface.

52
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Together, displays, alarms, point tables, and event history support daily operations.

53
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:18,000
CIM-EMS and the administration tools extend that picture to analysis and engineering.

54
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
This concludes the JSON-SCADA demo. The visuals are captures from the running demo system.
